Q: Electron IPC response is undefined A: <p>You're really close, it's just one small thing in your preload.</p>
<p>Your <code>performAction</code> calls <code>ipcRenderer.invoke</code> but never returns the promise it hands back:</p>
<pre><code>contextBridge.exposeInMainWorld('myApi', {
    performAction(data) {
        ipcRenderer.invoke('myChannel', data) // this promise gets thrown away
    }
})
</code></pre>
<p>So when you do <code>await window.myApi.performAction(...)</code> in your component, you're actually awaiting <code>undefined</code>, which resolves to <code>undefined</code>. That's why <code>response</code> comes back empty.</p>
<p>Add the <code>return</code>:</p>
<pre><code>contextBridge.exposeInMainWorld('myApi', {
    performAction(data) {
        return ipcRenderer.invoke('myChannel', data)
    }
})
</code></pre>
<p>That's the whole fix.</p>
<p>The reason you saw &quot;nothing happens, no error&quot; is that the invoke actually did fire and your main handler did run and return its string. You just dropped the promise in the bridge before the renderer could await it, so nothing threw and nothing showed up.</p>
<p>Everything else looks fine. Your handler is registered before <code>createWindow()</code>, and returning a plain string from <code>ipcMain.handle</code> is good since invoke wraps it in a promise for you. invoke/handle is the right pattern for a single request and response like this.</p>
